# Project Memory Records

Use `docs/ai-records/` for durable project history. Follow applicable repository instructions first. Missing records do not block ordinary work and do not require initialization for a read-only question.

## Retrieve only relevant history

- For a handoff or resumed task, read recent relevant entries in `CHANGE_LOG.md`.
- For a recurring issue, search `MISTAKE_INDEX.md` by topic, then read matching entries under `mistakes/`.
- When reconsidering an approach, search `FAILED_APPROACHES.md` for the approach or constraint concerned.
- Reuse unchanged material already read in this conversation. Do not read whole logs or every date file before each edit.

## Record a work unit

When the project maintains a change log, record a concise result once per completed work unit: date, objective, affected locations, validation and unresolved limitations. Long work or handoffs may benefit from an interim entry. Use truthful states including in progress, complete, blocked or abandoned; status names are not a gate.

Capture mistakes only when confirmed evidence produces a reusable lesson, not for routine experimentation. Add the symptom, cause, correction and prevention to `mistakes/YYYY-MM-DD.md`, then update the relevant topic entry in `MISTAKE_INDEX.md`.

Record a failed approach when it has been ruled out by evidence or a durable constraint. Include the reason and replacement or remaining question. Do not characterize an unfinished attempt as failed.

Create files lazily when writing an actual entry. Keep indexes concise and split large logs by topic or date; search them rather than requiring whole-file reads. Do not duplicate the same account in multiple logs.

## Completion

Requested history is accurate and discoverable; record only material lessons and work results. Logging does not authorize commits or extend a completed implementation into an architecture review.
